The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
list_for_each_entry(p, &sr->all_publ, all_publ)
    if (p->key == *last_key)
        break;
if (p->key != *last_key)
```
Reason:
The loop iterates over the entries in the list, and if it does not find a matching key, it checks `p->key` after the loop. If the loop exited without a break, `p` may not point to a valid entry, leading to dereferencing an invalid pointer, which can cause undefined behavior.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (list_entry_is_head(p, &sr->all_publ, all_publ))
    return -EPIPE;
```
Reason:
This change checks if `p` is the head of the list after the loop. If it is, it indicates that the loop did not find a valid entry, thus preventing the dereferencing of an invalid pointer and ensuring that the code only proceeds when `p` is valid.

